.TH AMAIL 1
.SH NAME
amail \- acme mail client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B amail
[
.B -f
.I mbox
] [
.B -m
.I mailbox
]
.br
.B amail
.B -t
[
.B -f
.I mbox
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Amail
is a mail client that runs inside
.IR acme (1),
after Plan 9's acme Mail.
It reads the mailbox from
.IR upas/fs (1)'s
.B /mail/fs
and shows the message list in an acme window whose tag carries
.B Get
and
.BR Quit .
Executing a message number in the list (chord or middle button) opens that
message in its own window;
.B Del
.I n
deletes message
.I n
through
.BR /mail/fs/ctl ,
and
.B Get
re-reads the mailbox.
All transport is the modern
.IR tls12 (2)-based
upas backend, so no plaintext POP3 is involved.
.PP
With
.B -f
it serves a local mbox; if
.B /mail/fs
is already served it is used directly.
.B -m
selects the mailbox name (default
.BR mbox ).
.SS Self-test
.B -t
runs a headless self-test used by
.IR tests :
it starts the backend, reads the mailbox, prints the message list and the
first message the way the acme windows would format them, and exits without
touching acme.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/acme/acme/bin/src/amail.b
.br
.B /tests/amail.sh
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR upas/fs (1),
.IR nedmail (1),
.IR wmmail (1)
.SH BUGS
The acme window and event handling follows the standard acme-client pattern
but, unlike the mailbox data layer, is not exercised by the headless test
(acme needs a display).
Opening a message is by executing its number; there is no
.B B3
look-to-open on the list line yet.
